federal investigators are trying to  figure out who made a wave of bomb  threats across the United States and  parts of Canada it turned out to be an  elaborate extortion hoax the email  threats demanded $20,000 in payments in  Bitcoin targets included government  buildings schools businesses and news  organizations from San Francisco to New  York Jeff Pegues is outside of the FBI  headquarters in Washington Jeff good  morning good morning the FBI is  assisting local law enforcement in  responding to all of these threats  they've been received in nearly every US  state it is unclear at this time how  many threatening emails were sent but in  New York City alone 30 to 40 were sent  to various locations the reports of bomb  threats spread like wildfire across the  country this bomb with mass evacuations  from coast to coast in Des Moines Iowa  police scramble to answer a flurry of  calls in about 40 minutes we had four  businesses here in town get a hold of us  and say that they'd received bomb  threats in San Francisco calls came in  from close to 20 locations the types of  businesses affected by these bomb  threats range from offices financial  institutions as the calls came in  officers and good jeopardy  bomb sniffing dogs soon followed but in  every city in small town the end result  was the same we have not found any  explosive devices or found any of the  threats to be credible CBS News has  learned that in recent weeks federal law  enforcement officials sent a bulletin to  local police departments across the  country warning of digital ransom  threats after 15 states receive similar  messages between August and November of  this year the very basic consequences to  an attack or even a threat like this is  that a cost business it cost government  it cost taxpayers a lot of time and  money  CNET senior producer Dan Patterson says  cyber criminals may be testing new  methods to see how we respond inevitably  at some point we will have to contend  with a cyber attack that harms or  damages a city may end up resulting in  the loss of life  but right now the priority for the FBI  is stopping the spread of these  threatening emails one threatening email  that was sent to a Missouri middle  school was traced to an IP address in  Moscow typically in these cases the  suspects are pretty good at covering  their tracks and leading investigators  down dead ends  Gale very disturbing stuff this morning  thank you Jeff
 

